== Summary ==

Evaluation and continuous improvement is the knowledge area concerned with
finding out whether a learning experience works, for whom, and under what
conditions — and feeding those findings back into the next design
iteration. It is what distinguishes learning ''engineering'' from
learning ''development'': the design loop is closed with evidence rather
than shipped and forgotten.

== Scope ==

In scope: defining success measures before building; instrumenting
learning experiences so the needed data exists; formative and summative
evaluation; analysis of learner outcomes and process data; and the
organizational practices that turn findings into design changes.

Out of scope: the design of the experiences themselves
([[KA5: Instructional Strategy Design]],
[[KA6: Learning Experience Design]]) and the general design of research
studies ([[KA11: Research Methods]]) — though the boundary with KA11 is
deliberately porous: evaluation borrows its rigor from research methods
and returns questions worth studying.

== Key concepts ==

* '''Formative vs. summative evaluation''' — improving a thing while it is being built vs. judging it once built.
* '''Instrumentation''' — designing what gets logged, and when, so that evaluation questions are answerable later.
* '''Learning analytics''' — analysis of learner-produced data to understand and optimize learning and its environments.
* '''Rapid experimentation''' — A/B tests and quasi-experiments embedded in running learning products.
* '''Improvement science''' — disciplined iteration cycles (e.g., plan–do–study–act) applied at program scale.
* '''Levels of evidence''' — matching the strength of a claim to the strength of its study design.

== Methods and practices ==

Practitioners in this KA write measurement plans alongside design
documents, build dashboards and data pipelines for learner data, run and
interpret embedded experiments, conduct efficacy studies, and run
retrospectives that convert findings into prioritized design changes.

